# Normalise line endings in the repository, not in the working tree.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# On Windows, `core.autocrlf=true` (the Git for Windows default) rewrites LF to CRLF in
# the working tree. That silently breaks this repository in CI and on any Linux/macOS
# machine that clones it:
#
#   setup-ansible.sh:1: /usr/bin/env bash^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# The script is invoked by Terraform's local-exec provisioner, so a CRLF shebang means
# `terraform apply` fails at the provisioning step — after the EC2 instances have already
# been created and billed.
#
# Declaring the line endings explicitly makes the repository correct regardless of each
# contributor's core.autocrlf setting.

# ── Default: text files, LF in the repo, native in the working tree ───────────
* text=auto eol=lf

# ── Must be LF in the working tree too, or they break on Linux ───────────────

# Bash scripts. A CRLF shebang or a stray \r in a variable assignment produces
# errors that are extremely hard to diagnose from the Terraform output.
*.sh      text eol=lf
setup-ansible.sh text eol=lf

# Terraform. Terraform is whitespace-sensitive in heredocs and provisioner commands.
*.tf      text eol=lf
*.tfvars  text eol=lf
*.tftest  text eol=lf
.terraform.lock.hcl text eol=lf

# Ansible. YAML forbids tabs for indentation and CRLF breaks `ansible-lint` diffs.
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf

# GitHub Actions. The runner always uses LF; a CRLF workflow file fails to parse.
.github/** text eol=lf

# Makefiles. A trailing \r after a recipe line makes the shell try to run "echo hello\r".
Makefile  text eol=lf
*.mk      text eol=lf

# Documentation and metadata.
*.md      text eol=lf
LICENSE   text eol=lf
.gitignore       text eol=lf
.gitattributes   text eol=lf

# ── Binary: never touch ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.ico     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.zip     binary
*.gz      binary
*.tfstate binary
*.tfplan  binary
